<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	MYR-246 — additive Info.plist. GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays YES; Xcode
	merges the generated keys (launch screen, UIUserInterfaceStyle, versions,
	privacy strings from INFOPLIST_KEY_* in project.yml) on top of this file.
	This file carries ONLY the keys that have no INFOPLIST_KEY_* equivalent —
	here, the myrobotaxi:// custom URL scheme the in-app Tesla link callback
	(§7.11 `myrobotaxi://tesla-linked`) — and, MYR-258, the owner car-offboarding
	consent-revoke return (§7.12 `myrobotaxi://tesla-unlinked`) — are intercepted
	on. Both callbacks share the single `myrobotaxi` scheme below (distinguished by
	host); ASWebAuthenticationSession matches on the scheme, so no second entry is
	needed.

	MYR-453 gives that same scheme a THIRD use — `myrobotaxi://join/{CODE}`, the
	invite link's fallback channel for when iOS declines to fire the universal
	link (a WKWebView-backed in-app browser, i.e. Telegram's default). It needs no
	new entry here either: CFBundleURLTypes registers the SCHEME, and the scheme
	is already ours. What was missing was a HANDLER — a `myrobotaxi://` URL
	arriving outside an active ASWebAuthenticationSession was silently discarded,
	because nothing in the app implemented `onOpenURL`. `RootView` does now, and
	routes it into the same `InviteLinkBridge` the universal link uses.

	The two Tesla hosts are unaffected: the session intercepts its own callback
	while it is running, and a stray `myrobotaxi://tesla-linked` with no session
	reaches `InviteLink.code(from:)`, fails its envelope, and is ignored — the
	same nothing that happened before.
	-->
	<!--
	MYR-172 — Live Activities. This key is what lets the app call
	`Activity.request` at all; without it ActivityKit reports Live Activities as
	unavailable and every request fails, with no build-time signal.

	It lives HERE rather than in project.yml's `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` block for the
	same reason CFBundleURLTypes does: there is no `INFOPLIST_KEY_` spelling for
	it, so the additive plist is the only place it can go.

	It is NOT an entitlement and needs no portal capability — which is why the
	widget extension's new provisioning profile carries nothing special. The
	APNs side of Live Activities rides the app's EXISTING `aps-environment`
	entitlement, because the push topic is the host app's bundle id with
	`.push-type.liveactivity` appended.
	-->
	<key>NSSupportsLiveActivities</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	Deliberately NOT declared: `NSSupportsLiveActivitiesFrequentUpdates`. That
	key asks the system to relax the update budget, and MYR-194 already fixed the
	cadence inside the ordinary budget — a push per lifecycle transition plus a
	60–90s ETA tick. Asking for frequent updates we do not send would only expose
	a Settings toggle the rider can disable, which is a new failure mode in
	exchange for nothing.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>app.myrobotaxi.ios.tesla-link</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>myrobotaxi</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
